# Code Quality (quality)

`quality` is the author-side quality gate MODE child of the `sk-code` family.
It runs after the acting agent applying `workflow-implement.md` changes the code and before the surface's verification workflow or done-claim.
It consumes the shared surface router, loads the right checklist for the detected surface and target path, fixes quality-gate failures in place, and leaves findings-only output to `sk-code-review`.

---

## 1. WHEN TO USE

### Activation Triggers

Use this mode when the request involves:
- Running the post-implementation quality gate before a completion claim.
- Applying P0/P1/P2 author-side checks to changed code, scripts, skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, or spec docs.
- Checking comment hygiene on modified files and removing ephemeral artifact references from comments while preserving durable WHY.
- Loading a SKILLED authoring checklist for `.skilled/skills/`, `.skilled/agents/`, `.skilled/commands/`, `specs/`, MCP server source, or language-specific SKILLED files.
- Fixing quality-gate failures in place with `Edit` when the fix belongs to the already-authored file.

Keyword triggers: `quality gate`, `code quality`, `comment hygiene`, `surface checklist`, `authoring checklist`, `SKILLED checklist`, `check before done`.

### When NOT to Use

Skip this mode when:
- The user needs code written, files scaffolded, or behavior implemented. Hand it to the acting agent applying `workflow-implement.md` for the detected surface.
- A failure needs symptom-to-root-cause investigation or a single-cause bug fix. Use the surface's debugging workflow (`workflow-debug.md`).
- The task is to collect final non-mutating verification evidence. Use the surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`).
- The user asks for findings-first review output, severity-ranked findings, or PR review. Use `sk-code-review`.
- The task is documentation-only prose with no code-work contract. Use `sk-doc`.

### Family Boundary

This is an independently invokable member of the `sk-code` family. It owns the quality gate, not implementation planning, debugging, verification evidence, or formal review reporting. It may edit files that the surface skill already changed to satisfy gate failures, but it does not create new files and does not dispatch subagents.

Pairs well with:
- The surface skill (`sk-code-skilled`) immediately before this gate, because implementation writes the files this mode checks.
- The surface's debugging workflow (`workflow-debug.md`) when a quality failure reveals a functional bug or failing command.
- The surface's verification workflow (`workflow-verify.md`) after P0 quality issues are clear, because verification gates the final claim.
- `sk-code-review` when the user wants findings-only output rather than author-side correction.

---

## 2. SMART ROUTING

The `sk-code` hub routes a post-implementation quality gate here: the request names a quality gate, comment hygiene, an authoring checklist or a check before done, and the hub's `quality` signal selects this packet.
This mode routes by target path more than by keyword, because the checklists a change needs follow the files it touches.
`node scripts/quality-gate.cjs --explain <path>` names those checklists and why, and its answer outranks any keyword match.

Three resources load first: `references/runner-contract.md` for the runner's CLI, gate values and verdict;
`../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` for the shared severity floor; and `references/code-style-guide.md` for this packet's style and comment rules.
The resource domains, loading levels, path-keyed rows and comment-hygiene layers follow in the next section.

The prompt-intent projection in that router only makes the default resources scoreable; it never picks the path-keyed checklist.
Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.

---

## 3. RESOURCES

### Resource Domains

- This mode ships no house-style checklist. The universal floor and the universal style guide apply on every surface, and each surface's own checklists carry its house style.
  `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` is the SKILLED baseline, loaded when SKILLED is a resolved surface of the target, and the SKILLED JavaScript and CSS house checks sit under `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/`.
- `../sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/` contains OpenCode authoring checklists for skills, agents, commands, MCP servers, language files, and config; the spec-folder checklist is owned by `system-spec-kit`.
- `scripts/quality-gate.cjs` is the runner. It reads the change, runs the built-in checks, probes the enforcement layers, lists the checklists the declarations name and prints one verdict. It writes no file.
  The only commands it runs beyond its own are the ones a repository's committed `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` names, each as an argv array under a deadline and the workspace guard, and only once the repository is trusted (`references/quality-config.md` section 8).
  A command a manifest implies is reported as a suggestion it never runs.
  `references/runner-contract.md` holds its CLI, gate values, verdict, envelope grammar and rule ids, and `references/quality-config.md` holds the repository config: its schema, discovery order, merge rule and trust boundary.
- `assets/quality-defaults.json` holds the runner's base layer: excluded paths, sensor settings and the checklist rows this mode owns. It names no command.
- `scripts/check-comment-hygiene.sh` is the per-file comment-hygiene gate. The runner calls it once per run with `--json`.
- `scripts/check-dist-staleness.sh` checks generated/distribution artifact drift when that is part of the target.
- `scripts/ceiling-report.sh [<file>...]` lists the `ceiling:` and `intentional-limit:` comment markers and tags the ones with no trigger or no measurable signal.
  Run it for a debt pass, before a release or when a reviewer asks what limits the code knowingly accepts.
  It reads every tracked code file, or only the files named, prints one `path:line  text  [tags]` line per marker and a closing `markers=N no-trigger=N no-signal=N` line, and exits 0 because it reports and does not gate.
- `scripts/hooks/claude-posttooluse.sh` is the legacy write-time hook, kept for direct tests and registered in no runtime. The live write-time warning is `.skilled/hooks/post-edit-quality/claude/claude-posttooluse.cjs`.
- `../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md` is the cross-mode severity standard this packet consumes, and `references/code-style-guide.md` is this packet's own style and comment-hygiene guide.

